body:
Can confirm this issue in 24d7451

After poking around in DisplayServer, I noticed that the main thread gets blocked in DisplayServerWindows::embed_process() if the child process is hung during the SetWindowPos() invocation, causing Windows to report that the editor is not responding.

Trying to send a null message to the child process with SendMessageTimeout() to check if it is responsive before we invoke SetWindowPos() can indeed keep the main thread going, and the output console can happily print logs. However, the editor still won't respond to any mouse click events (such as clicking the stop button to kill the child process), indicating the need for a deeper dive into the codebase.

Here's a little patch I created to test things out:

```diff
diff --git a/platform/windows/display_server_windows.cpp b/platform/windows/display_server_windows.cpp
index d6e4063d..fcd55d18 100644
--- a/platform/windows/display_server_windows.cpp
+++ b/platform/windows/display_server_windows.cpp
@@ -2936,6 +2936,14 @@ Error DisplayServerWindows::embed_process(WindowID p_window, OS::ProcessID p_pid
 		embedded_processes.insert(p_pid, ep);
 	}

+	// Try sending a message to the embed process to see if it's responsive before invocation.
+	// We can return early to prevent main thread from hanging at the SetWindowPos().
+	BOOL is_responsive = SendMessageTimeout(ep->window_handle, WM_NULL, 0, 0, SMTO_ABORTIFHUNG, 1000, nullptr);
+	if (!is_responsive) {
+		ERR_PRINT(vformat("Embed process %s is unresponsive.", p_pid));
+		return ERR_BUSY;
+	}
+
 	if (p_rect.size.x <= 100 || p_rect.size.y <= 100) {
 		p_visible = false;
 	}
```

And here's the screenshot I took of running a infinite loop with the patch, as described. Notice that the error log is printed at the bottom, and the parent process remains responsive (Windows not reporting "not responding") while the child process is hung.

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/72bfa6e0-fa55-44da-ab08-2a1679e6b6a7)

You could try comment out the `return ERR_BUSY;` line for comparison. It should act as the original behavior, only a log will be printed.

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1fec43ed-87cf-4ac4-852d-5ffeed7d534e)

Hope this can provide some hints for addressing the issue. I'll try to dig a little further to see if I can come up with a full solution.

After extensive documentation reading and some more headscratches, I finally made a working prototype to prevent the editor from freezing due to the embedded game being frozen. The reason the editor doesn't respond to mouse click events, even though the main thread isn't blocked, is that the message queue remains obstructed by the frozen child window.

I refined the idea I shared a few days ago, that is, to detach the embedded window from the editor window whenever a hang is detected. This frees up the editor window from the clogged message queue it shares with the embedded game, so the editor can handle Windows messages like the game is not embedded.

Here’s the working patch:
```diff
diff --git a/platform/windows/display_server_windows.cpp b/platform/windows/display_server_windows.cpp
index d6e4063d..0bed143b 100644
--- a/platform/windows/display_server_windows.cpp
+++ b/platform/windows/display_server_windows.cpp
@@ -2936,6 +2936,19 @@ Error DisplayServerWindows::embed_process(WindowID p_window, OS::ProcessID p_pid
 		embedded_processes.insert(p_pid, ep);
 	}

+	// Try sending a message to the embed process to see if it's responsive.
+	// We can return early to prevent main thread from hanging.
+	// Also, we should detach child window so that the parent window is freed from the child window message queue.
+	BOOL is_responsive = SendMessageTimeout(ep->window_handle, WM_NULL, 0, 0, SMTO_ABORTIFHUNG, 1000, nullptr);
+	if (!is_responsive) {
+		ERR_PRINT(vformat("Embed process %s is unresponsive for 1000ms.", p_pid));
+		SetWindowLongPtr(ep->window_handle, GWLP_HWNDPARENT, (LONG_PTR) nullptr);
+		return ERR_BUSY;
+	}
+
+	// Ensure that the embed process window is child of editor window.
+	SetWindowLongPtr(ep->window_handle, GWLP_HWNDPARENT, (LONG_PTR) ep->parent_window_handle);
+
 	if (p_rect.size.x <= 100 || p_rect.size.y <= 100) {
 		p_visible = false;
 	}
```

Here’s a demo screen capture where you can see me clicking the stop button to kill the hanging embedded game after applying the patch:

https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/acbb1791-09a8-4e8c-8843-f46cfc5e72ef

But there are still some caveats. Sometimes the responsiveness detection code can produce false negatives, allowing the editor’s main thread to be blocked by some Win32 APIs like the SetWindowPos() that uses SendMessage() underneath (pretty rare tho). This concept will need a bit more work to fully resolve the issue.

I got the idea to detach the window from a discussion among the Chromium devs about the input queue in this conversation: [Isolating the browser process from third-party accessibility software](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/chromium-dev/c/_9jq1ovNF9o/m/st9Q3vVxCQAJ). The "Old hang resistance" section by Michael Ens is especially helpful and overall quite informative about this issue.

In addition, this post by Raymond Chen is quite helpful: [I warned you: The dangers of attaching input queues](https://devblogs.microsoft.com/oldnewthing/20080801-00/?p=21393). I’d say there are a few more posts by Raymond that are really useful, but this is the one I’d recommend if someone wants to take a deeper dive into input queues.
